-- 10 original IELTS Academic reading passages (6 paragraphs, 700+ words each).
-- Each passage carries 25 questions: 5 Multiple Choice (3 single, 2 multiple),
-- 5 True/False/Not Given, 5 Yes/No/Not Given, 5 Matching Information and
-- 5 Sentence Completion. That is 50 new questions per reading subtask.
-- Topics, titles and questions were checked against every IELTS and PTE reading
-- passage in the seed migrations removed in 76e414e, and do not repeat them.
-- Replay preserves editorial changes. All IDs use the ir67 namespace.

-- Later Starts: Teenagers and the School Day
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-sleep', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Later Starts: Teenagers and the School Day', 'Why adolescent body clocks clash with early timetables',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "Anyone who has tried to wake a teenager for school will recognise the scene: an alarm that rings unanswered, a body that seems welded to the mattress, and a breakfast eaten, if at all, on the way out of the door. Sleep specialists generally advise that people aged between thirteen and eighteen need between eight and ten hours of sleep a night, yet surveys in many countries suggest that a large proportion of secondary students regularly get far less than this. Adults tend to offer a familiar explanation. Teenagers, they say, stay up too late with their phones, their games and their friends, and then complain about the consequences in the morning. There is some truth in this account, but it leaves out the most important part of the story, which concerns not habits but biology. The difficulty begins long before the phone is picked up."}, {"label": "B", "text": "During puberty, the internal clock that governs when we feel sleepy shifts to a later time. The change is driven partly by melatonin, a hormone that signals to the body that night has arrived. In adolescents, melatonin tends to be released later in the evening than it is in children or adults, so a fifteen-year-old who lies awake at ten o''clock is often not being stubborn or lazy; their body is simply not yet ready for sleep. A second process adds to the effect. Throughout the waking day, a need for sleep, known as sleep pressure, gradually builds up. Research suggests that in teenagers this pressure accumulates more slowly than it does in younger children, which makes it easier for them to remain alert late into the evening. Together, these changes push the natural bedtime of many adolescents towards eleven o''clock or even later."}, {"label": "C", "text": "When a school day begins at half past seven or eight o''clock, it collides with this biological timetable. Students who must rise at six are, in effect, being asked to function in the middle of their biological night. The results are visible in the classroom: tired students find it harder to pay attention, their mood is more likely to suffer, and the sleep they lose may include the stages during which the brain consolidates what was learned the previous day. Teachers often report that the first lesson of the day is the hardest to teach, with some pupils barely able to keep their eyes open. The risks are not confined to school buildings. Newly licensed drivers are among the most vulnerable road users, and drowsiness adds to that danger on early morning journeys. For some students, a lack of sleep also appears to be linked with poorer mental health."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In response, some medical organisations have recommended that secondary schools should start no earlier than 8.30 in the morning, and a number of school districts have moved their timetables accordingly. Studies of these changes have generally found that students slept longer on school nights, because they woke later while their bedtimes changed relatively little. Several districts also reported better attendance and fewer late arrivals. The changes were not free, however. In many areas the same fleet of buses carries pupils to both primary and secondary schools, one group after the other, so moving one start time affects the whole bus schedule. Later finishing times can squeeze after-school sports and part-time jobs, and some parents found that the new timetable no longer fitted around their own working hours. These practical objections, rather than any doubt about the science, have been the main reason why many schools have not changed."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Critics of later start times often predict that teenagers will simply stay up later, cancelling out any benefit. In my reading of the evidence, this fear has not been borne out: the extra sleep gained in the morning is largely kept. That does not mean a later start is a cure-all. A student who takes a phone to bed and scrolls until two in the morning will remain exhausted whatever time the first bell rings, and schools that change their timetables without also talking to families about sleep habits are likely to be disappointed. Screens, caffeine and heavy homework loads all play a part. A later start removes one obstacle, but only one, and it would be a mistake to present it to parents as the whole answer to the problem of tired adolescents."}, {"label": "F", "text": "How, then, should schools decide? Decisions of this kind are best taken locally, after consultation with parents, teachers, students and transport providers, rather than imposed from above by national governments that cannot see the particular constraints of each area. The transport problems are real, but they are problems of organisation, and organisation can be changed. What must change first is the attitude that pupils'' sleep is a private lifestyle matter for families alone. Sleep is directly connected with attention, memory and behaviour, and schools should therefore treat it as a learning issue in the same way that they treat nutrition or reading. The most sensible approach is to introduce any new timetable for a trial period, measuring attendance, punctuality and results before and after, so that the decision rests on evidence rather than on habit."}]'::jsonb,
     856, 'medium', 'Education and health', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

