-- Farming Upwards
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-vertical', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Farming Upwards', 'The promise and the price of growing food indoors',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "In a converted warehouse on the edge of a city, lettuces grow in trays stacked ten or twelve layers high, lit not by the sun but by rows of purple-pink LED lamps. There is no soil. Instead, the plants'' roots hang in shallow channels of water to which nutrients have been added, a method known as hydroponics. Temperature, humidity and even the concentration of carbon dioxide in the air are controlled by computer. This is vertical farming, an idea popularised at the end of the twentieth century by an American professor of public health and his students, who imagined skyscrapers feeding entire cities. The warehouse version is less dramatic than their vision, but the principle is the same: to grow food indoors, close to the people who will eat it, in a space that uses height rather than land."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Supporters point to a long list of advantages. Because crops are stacked, the yield from each square metre of floor space can be many times higher than that of an open field. Because the growing rooms are sealed, insects are kept out and pesticides are unnecessary. Water that is not taken up by the plants is collected, filtered and recycled, so a vertical farm uses only a small fraction of the water needed to grow the same crop outdoors. Production continues all year round, and it is not affected by the weather: drought, frost and flooding, which can destroy a harvest in the field, have no effect inside a building. Finally, since the farms can be located in or near cities, the produce travels a short distance and can reach shops within hours of being picked."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The central difficulty is energy. On an ordinary farm, sunlight is free; in a vertical farm, every unit of light that a plant uses must be generated by electricity. Lighting, together with the air-conditioning needed to remove the heat that the lamps produce, accounts for a very large share of operating costs. LED technology has become far more efficient over the past two decades, and lamps can now be tuned to the wavelengths that plants use most. Despite these improvements, a vertical farm still uses far more energy per kilogram of lettuce than a greenhouse, which makes use of natural light and needs artificial lighting only in the darkest months. When electricity prices rose sharply in the early 2020s, several well-funded vertical-farming companies in Europe and North America closed or went bankrupt."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The choice of crops reflects these economics. Almost all vertical farms grow leafy greens, herbs and microgreens. Such plants grow quickly, are small enough to fit on closely spaced shelves, and are almost entirely edible, so little of the energy spent on them is wasted on stalks or roots. They also command high prices per kilogram and are easily damaged in transport, which gives local production an advantage. Staple crops are a different matter. Wheat, rice and maize need far more light to produce their grain, occupy more space, and sell for a very low price per kilogram. Researchers have calculated that growing wheat indoors would be many times more expensive than growing it in a field. For the foreseeable future, therefore, vertical farms cannot supply the calories on which most of the world''s diet depends."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Much of the industry''s recent trouble, in my view, was self-inflicted. Early promoters spoke as if vertical farming would replace conventional agriculture and feed the growing cities of the world, and investors who believed them were bound to be disappointed. The technology is better understood as a useful niche than as a revolution. The same caution applies to one of the industry''s favourite slogans. Vegetables grown next to a supermarket may indeed have ''zero food miles'', but the phrase is misleading if the electricity used to grow them came from burning fossil fuels. A lettuce that travels a thousand kilometres by lorry from a sunny field may, in some cases, have a smaller carbon footprint than one grown under lamps around the corner. Honest comparisons would serve the industry better than slogans."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Where, then, does vertical farming make most sense? The most promising projects are those that pair growing rooms with renewable electricity, or with waste heat from factories and power stations that would otherwise be lost. The technology also has obvious value in places where conventional agriculture is difficult: in deserts, where water is scarce, and in cold, remote communities, where fresh vegetables must otherwise be flown in at great cost. Public money has a role to play, but it should be spent carefully. Rather than subsidising individual companies, governments would do better to fund research into more efficient lighting and growing systems, whose results could be shared across the whole sector. If vertical farming is to have a lasting future, it will be built on modest claims and careful engineering, not on promises to transform the world''s food supply."}]'::jsonb,
     814, 'medium', 'Agriculture and technology', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

