-- Drinking the Sea
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-desal', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Drinking the Sea', 'How desalination works, and what it costs the ocean',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "Around ninety-seven per cent of the water on Earth is found in the oceans, and it is too salty to drink or to use on most crops. As populations grow and droughts become more frequent, a growing number of coastal cities have decided that the sea itself must become a source of drinking water. The process of removing salt from seawater is called desalination, and in its simplest form it is very old. Sailors on long voyages learned to boil seawater and collect the steam, which condensed into fresh water as it cooled. The principle is sound, but the method consumes a great deal of fuel for a small amount of water, and for centuries it was used only in emergencies. Turning desalination into a reliable supply for millions of people required both new technology and a willingness to pay for it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The first large-scale plants used thermal distillation, an industrial version of the sailors'' technique. Seawater is heated, often in a series of chambers at progressively lower pressures, so that it boils at lower temperatures and some of the heat can be reused. The vapour is then condensed as pure water, leaving the salt behind. Distillation is robust and copes well with very salty or dirty seawater, but it requires far more energy than the membrane method described below. It became the dominant technology in the Gulf states, where fuel was abundant and cheap, and where plants were often built alongside power stations so that their surplus heat could be put to use. In regions without cheap fuel, however, distillation was rarely affordable, and the search for a less energy-hungry process continued."}, {"label": "C", "text": "That process is reverse osmosis. Seawater is pumped at high pressure against a semi-permeable membrane, a thin sheet whose pores allow water molecules to pass while holding back the dissolved salt. Reverse osmosis is now used in most newly built desalination plants around the world. Its energy consumption has fallen considerably, partly because of energy recovery devices, which capture the pressure remaining in the salty water that leaves the membranes and use it to pressurise the incoming flow. The membranes themselves, however, are delicate. If seawater is fed to them directly, algae and sediment quickly block the pores, a problem known as fouling. Plants therefore include pre-treatment stages that filter the water before it reaches the membranes, and the membranes must be cleaned regularly and eventually replaced."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Every desalination plant produces a waste product as well as fresh water. For each litre of drinking water, a typical seawater plant releases more than a litre of brine, which contains roughly twice the salt concentration of ordinary seawater along with traces of the chemicals used in treatment. Because brine is denser than seawater, it tends to sink and spread across the seabed, where it can harm animals and plants that cannot move away. Engineers reduce the damage by releasing brine through diffusers, pipes with many outlets that mix it rapidly with the surrounding sea. The intake is a second concern. Large pipes drawing in seawater can trap fish, larvae and plankton. Modern plants reduce this by drawing water slowly through fine screens, or by taking it from wells beneath the seabed, where sand acts as a natural filter."}, {"label": "E", "text": "It would be foolish to dismiss desalination. For a city that has watched its reservoirs empty during a long drought, a plant that can draw on the sea regardless of rainfall is a valuable insurance policy. My concern is that it is increasingly being used as a way of avoiding harder choices. In many cities, a large proportion of treated water is lost through leaking pipes before it reaches a single tap, and repairing those pipes would usually provide water far more cheaply than building a new plant. Similarly, water pricing that rewards careful use could reduce demand at almost no cost to the public purse. Desalination is attractive to politicians partly because it is visible and dramatic; mending pipes and reforming tariffs are not. Yet a city that builds a plant without first tackling waste is simply paying a high price to lose water more expensively."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The technology will continue to improve. Several plants are now powered wholly or partly by solar energy, which reduces both their running costs and their emissions. Researchers are also investigating whether brine, rather than being a waste product, could become a resource. It contains valuable minerals, including magnesium and lithium, the latter in high demand for the batteries used in electric vehicles and phones. Whether such extraction will ever be economic on a large scale is still uncertain. I am cautiously optimistic about these developments, but optimism is not a substitute for scrutiny. The environmental effects of each plant should be monitored by independent bodies rather than by the companies that operate them, and the results should be published, so that the public can judge whether the promised protections are working."}]'::jsonb,
     817, 'medium', 'Water and environment', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

