-- Keepers of the Light
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-lighthouse', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Keepers of the Light', 'From coastal bonfires to automated beacons',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "For as long as ships have sailed close to dangerous coasts, people have tried to warn them with light. The most famous early example, the Pharos of Alexandria, was completed in the third century BCE and was counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World; a fire burned at its summit, and it stood for well over a thousand years. Most early coastal lights were far humbler: fires lit on hilltops or on simple towers, often burning wood and, later, coal in open iron baskets. Such fires were a poor guide. They consumed enormous quantities of fuel, and in wind and rain they flared unpredictably or went out altogether. Their smoke could blacken the surroundings and, on some nights, hide the flames it came from, so that sailors could never be sure whether a dark coast was safe or simply unlit."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The eighteenth century brought a series of improvements. Candles and simple oil lamps were placed in lantern rooms at the top of towers, protected from the weather by glass. Their light was weak, but it could be strengthened by placing reflectors made of polished metal behind the flame, curved so that they gathered light and sent it out towards the sea. A more important advance came in the 1780s, when the Swiss scientist Aimé Argand designed a lamp with a hollow, circular wick. Air flowed up through the centre of the wick as well as around its outside, so the flame burned hotter and far more brightly, and produced much less smoke. Argand lamps combined with reflectors became the standard equipment of lighthouses for several decades, although the reflectors needed constant polishing to remain effective."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The decisive innovation, however, was a new kind of lens. In 1822 the French physicist Augustin Fresnel presented a design that replaced a single thick lens with concentric rings of glass prisms arranged around a central lens. Each ring bent the light by the right amount, so that rays which would otherwise have escaped upwards or downwards were redirected into a concentrated horizontal beam. The result was far thinner and lighter than a solid lens of the same size, and dramatically more powerful. A good Fresnel lens could project a beam that was visible more than thirty kilometres away. Lenses were manufactured in several standard sizes, known as ''orders'', from the huge first-order lenses used on major coastal lights to small ones suitable for harbours, and many remained in service for more than a century."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A light is only useful if sailors know which light they are looking at. As the number of lighthouses grew, each was given a distinctive pattern of flashes, called its characteristic: one might flash white every five seconds, another show two red flashes followed by darkness. Rotating lenses, turned by clockwork, made such patterns possible. Some lights also showed coloured sectors, appearing red or green to a ship approaching from a dangerous direction and white from a safe one. By day, lighthouses were recognised by their shape and by daymarks, painted patterns such as stripes, bands or checks that distinguished one tower from its neighbours. Since light was useless in thick fog, many stations were also equipped with foghorns, whose own patterns of blasts identified them as clearly as flashes did at night."}, {"label": "E", "text": "All of this depended on keepers. Their work was repetitive and exacting: trimming the wicks of the lamps so that they did not smoke, winding the clockwork that rotated the lens, polishing the glass and brass, and recording weather, passing ships and any failures in the station''s logbooks. At shore stations, keepers often lived with their families in cottages beside the tower. On offshore rock stations, by contrast, men served for weeks at a time, separated from their families and dependent on supply boats that could be delayed for days by storms. The popular image of the solitary keeper, gazing heroically out to sea, is in my view a romantic distortion. The job demanded patience and routine far more than heroism, and it is that dull reliability, rather than drama, which saved lives."}, {"label": "F", "text": "During the twentieth century, electric lamps replaced oil, and automatic equipment gradually made keepers unnecessary; in many countries the last of them left their towers in the 1980s and 1990s. Today ships navigate largely by satellite, and it is tempting to conclude that lighthouses have had their day. I believe this would be a mistake. Electronic systems can fail or be disrupted, and a visible light remains an independent check that requires no power on board the ship. What concerns me more is the fate of the buildings. Many redundant lighthouses have been sold and converted into holiday cottages to which the public has no access. Such conversions may keep the structures standing, but they are not the best form of preservation. Lighthouses are part of a shared maritime heritage, and at least some part of each should remain open to visitors."}]'::jsonb,
     820, 'medium', 'History and technology', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

