-- Listening Beneath the Waves
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-oceannoise', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Listening Beneath the Waves', 'The growing problem of noise in the ocean',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "In 1956 a celebrated documentary about undersea exploration was released under the title The Silent World, and for a long time that phrase captured how most people imagined the ocean. In reality, the sea is full of sound. Sound travels about four and a half times faster in water than in air, and it can carry over great distances with relatively little loss of energy. Light, by contrast, fades rapidly with depth; below a few hundred metres the ocean is almost completely dark. For this reason, many marine animals rely on sound rather than sight to find food, avoid predators, navigate and communicate. Whales, dolphins and many fish produce and interpret sounds with remarkable precision, and for them the acoustic environment of the ocean is as important as the visual landscape is to animals on land."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Much of the ocean''s sound is natural. Breaking waves, falling rain and the movement of ice all contribute a constant background hiss, while earthquakes and underwater volcanoes produce occasional deep rumbles. Living things add their own voices. Snapping shrimp, which make a loud crack by closing an enlarged claw at great speed, are so numerous in some warm, shallow waters that they dominate the soundscape. The most remarkable sounds of all belong to the great baleen whales. Their low-frequency calls can travel hundreds of kilometres, allowing individuals separated by vast distances to remain in contact. Coral reefs, too, have a distinctive sound, a chorus of crackles, grunts and pops produced by the many animals that live on them, and a healthy reef can be surprisingly noisy."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Over the past century, human activity has added a new layer of sound. The largest single source is commercial shipping. As a ship''s propeller turns, it creates regions of low pressure in which small bubbles form and then collapse violently, a process called cavitation, which generates intense noise. Much of this noise is low in frequency, overlapping with the range used by baleen whales. Other sources are more intermittent but louder. Surveys searching for oil and gas beneath the seabed use air guns, which release powerful pulses of compressed air every few seconds for weeks at a time. Naval sonar emits strong signals to detect submarines, and the construction of offshore wind farms often involves pile-driving, in which huge steel columns are hammered into the seabed."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The effects on marine life take several forms. The most widespread is masking, which occurs when background noise drowns out the signals that animals are trying to hear. Whales in noisy areas have been observed calling more loudly, repeating their calls, or falling silent until a ship has passed. Measurements have also found higher levels of stress hormones in whales exposed to heavy shipping traffic. Some effects are more subtle. The larvae of many reef fish drift in the open ocean before returning to settle on a reef, and experiments suggest that they use the sounds of healthy reefs to find a home; a reef drowned out by boat noise may attract fewer of them. The most dramatic cases involve military sonar, which has been linked to mass strandings in which the animals most often involved have been beaked whales."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Unlike many forms of pollution, noise has one great advantage from the point of view of those trying to reduce it: it stops as soon as its source stops. There is no residue to clean up and nothing accumulates in the food chain. Some remedies are therefore remarkably simple. Because cavitation increases sharply with speed, even a modest reduction in the speed of ships can reduce the noise they make considerably, while also saving fuel. Propellers and hulls can be designed to be quieter, and existing vessels can be maintained so that damaged propellers do not add unnecessary noise. During construction at sea, curtains of air bubbles released around pile-driving sites absorb part of the sound before it spreads. Seasonal restrictions can also keep the noisiest activities away from important breeding or feeding areas at critical times."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Ocean noise has been neglected, I would argue, largely because it is invisible. A plastic bag on a beach or an oil slick on the water makes a powerful photograph; a sound that fills an ocean basin does not. International guidance on reducing noise from ships exists, but it is voluntary, and I believe that noise limits should become a normal part of shipping regulations, alongside rules on fuel and emissions. There are good grounds for optimism, precisely because noise can be removed so quickly once action is taken. However, targets must be based on data. At present, long-term measurements exist for only a few parts of the ocean, and setting limits before we know how noise levels are changing would risk either demanding too much or achieving too little."}]'::jsonb,
     795, 'medium', 'Marine environment', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

