-- The Science of Waiting
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-queues', 'ielts-2026-01', 'The Science of Waiting', 'What mathematics and psychology reveal about queues',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "Most people spend a surprising amount of their lives waiting: at supermarket checkouts, on the telephone, outside doctors'' surgeries and in lines of traffic. The scientific study of waiting began in an unexpected place. In the early twentieth century, the Danish engineer Agner Krarup Erlang, who worked for the Copenhagen telephone company, set out to calculate how many lines and operators a telephone exchange needed. Calls arrived at unpredictable moments and lasted for unpredictable lengths of time, yet Erlang showed that their overall behaviour could be described mathematically. His work founded what became known as queuing theory, a branch of mathematics now used to design everything from call centres and hospital emergency departments to airport security and computer networks. Its central question is always the same: how much capacity is needed to keep waiting within acceptable limits?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "One of the most important lessons of queuing theory is that waiting times do not rise evenly as a system gets busier. When a service is lightly used, a small increase in customers makes little difference. As it approaches full capacity, however, waiting times begin to climb steeply, because there is no longer any slack to absorb the natural bunching of arrivals. A checkout that is busy ninety-five per cent of the time will have far longer queues than one that is busy eighty per cent of the time, even though the difference in demand seems small. For this reason, well-run services rarely operate at full capacity. They deliberately keep some spare capacity, such as an extra counter that can open when needed, accepting that staff will sometimes be idle in exchange for avoiding sudden, lengthy queues."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Yet the length of a wait is only part of the story, because the experience of waiting depends heavily on psychology. In an influential essay published in 1985, the service researcher David Maister set out a series of principles. Unoccupied time, he argued, feels longer than occupied time; uncertain waits feel longer than known, finite ones; unexplained waits feel longer than explained ones; and unfair waits feel longer than equitable ones. Anxiety, too, makes time pass slowly. A frequently told story illustrates the first principle. The managers of an office building, faced with complaints about slow lifts, are said to have installed mirrors beside the lift doors, after which the complaints stopped, because people were occupied looking at themselves. The story is hard to verify, and it proves little on its own, but it has become a favourite example in business schools."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Such principles have shaped the design of queues. Many banks, post offices and airports now use a single serpentine line that winds back and forth and feeds several counters, rather than a separate line in front of each counter. The single line looks longer, but it is fairer, because customers are served strictly in order of arrival and nobody is delayed by choosing the wrong line. It also reduces the variation in waiting times. Supermarkets have been slower to adopt the idea, partly because of limited space, and their customers are familiar with the feeling that the other line is always moving faster. Some organisations have removed physical lines altogether. Theme parks and restaurants increasingly offer virtual queues, in which customers are given a place in line and then receive a text message when it is their turn, leaving them free to do something else in the meantime."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Information matters as well. Displays showing estimated waiting times reduce uncertainty, and research on queues suggests that people become more tolerant of a long wait when they know how long it will be. Some theme park operators are reported to deliberately overstate displayed waiting times, so that visitors who expected to wait forty minutes are pleased to reach the front after thirty. The ending of an experience also has a disproportionate effect on how it is remembered. Psychologists have found that people''s memories of an experience are strongly influenced by its final moments, so a wait that ends with a friendly welcome and prompt service may be recalled more favourably than a shorter wait that ends in confusion. For organisations, this means that the last few minutes of a customer''s wait deserve particular attention."}, {"label": "F", "text": "There is, however, something uncomfortable about much of this. Organisations have become skilled at managing how waiting feels, and in my view they spend too much effort on such perception tricks and too little on reducing the waits themselves. Mirrors and entertaining screens are cheaper than extra staff, but they do not give anyone their time back. Overstating waiting times is, I believe, simply dishonest, even if it produces satisfied customers; accurate information is the foundation of trust. And the stakes are not equal everywhere. A long queue at a theme park is an irritation, but waiting for treatment in a hospital can affect a person''s health, and it is in public services of this kind that the careful study of waiting matters most. Queuing theory, after all, began as an attempt to make a service work better, not merely to make its failures easier to bear."}]'::jsonb,
     845, 'medium', 'Society and psychology', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

