-- Building with Grass
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-bamboo', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Building with Grass', 'The strengths and weaknesses of bamboo as a construction material',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "Bamboo is often described as a tree, but it is in fact a grass, and one of the fastest-growing plants on Earth. Some species can grow several tens of centimetres in a single day during their growing season, and a stem can reach its full height within a few months. A bamboo plantation can be harvested after three to five years, whereas timber from most trees takes decades to mature, and because the plant regrows from its underground root system, it does not need to be replanted after each harvest. People in Asia, Africa and Latin America have built with bamboo for thousands of years. In Colombia, for example, a large native species known locally as guadua has long been used for houses and bridges, and it has become the basis of a distinctive modern architecture."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The qualities that make bamboo useful to builders are closely connected with its structure. The stem, known as a culm, is a hollow tube divided at intervals by solid nodes, which stiffen it and prevent it from buckling. Its fibres run along its length, giving it great strength in tension; relative to its weight, this strength is often compared favourably with that of steel. Bamboo is also light and flexible. When the ground shakes, a bamboo frame tends to bend rather than break, and because the structure is light, it generates smaller forces during an earthquake than heavy masonry does. Bamboo houses have survived earthquakes that destroyed brick and concrete buildings nearby. These properties, combined with its low cost and wide availability, explain why so many architects and engineers have become interested in the material."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Bamboo does, however, have serious weaknesses. Its stems contain starch, which attracts beetles and other insects, and in damp conditions it is readily attacked by fungi. Untreated bamboo used outdoors may last only a few years before it begins to rot. Durability can be greatly improved by treatment. The most common method involves soaking the culms in a solution of borax and boric acid, which makes them unattractive to insects and fungi and is relatively safe to handle. Design matters too. Builders who work with bamboo often say that a building needs ''good boots and a good hat'': the bamboo must be raised above the ground on a foundation of stone or concrete, so that it does not absorb moisture from the soil, and protected by a roof with wide overhangs, so that rain does not reach the walls."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Engineering with bamboo presents its own difficulties. Unlike sawn timber, which can be cut to standard dimensions, bamboo is a natural product whose diameter and wall thickness vary from one culm to the next and even along the length of a single stem. Joints are especially problematic. Bamboo splits easily, so it cannot simply be nailed like wood, and traditional builders therefore connected poles using lashing, tying them together with rope or plant fibres. Modern engineers have developed stronger methods, such as passing steel bolts through the culm and filling the sections around them with mortar so that they do not crush. Another approach avoids the problem of irregular shapes altogether. In engineered bamboo, the culms are split into strips, which are then glued and pressed into boards and beams of standard sizes, much like laminated timber."}, {"label": "E", "text": "For many years, the absence of building codes held bamboo back. Engineers were reluctant to design with a material for which there were no agreed rules, and insurers and banks were reluctant to support buildings that had not been designed according to recognised standards. International standards for structural bamboo now exist, but many countries have not yet adopted them in their national regulations. Perception is a further obstacle. In regions where bamboo has traditionally been used for the homes of the rural poor, it is often dismissed as ''poor man''s timber'', and families who can afford brick or concrete tend to choose them as a sign of progress, even where a well-designed bamboo building would perform better."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Enthusiasts often present bamboo as a simple answer to the environmental costs of construction, but its benefits deserve careful examination. Bamboo shipped from Asia to Europe or North America loses much of its advantage over local timber during the journey, and engineered bamboo products depend on adhesives, some of which are derived from fossil fuels. In my view, bamboo is most valuable where it grows locally and can be used with little processing. It will not, and should not, replace timber everywhere. Changing its image is important, and I believe that high-profile buildings by well-known architects can do a great deal to show that bamboo is a material of choice rather than of necessity. Ultimately, though, the key is not new products but the training of local builders, who need the skills to treat, protect and join bamboo properly."}]'::jsonb,
     803, 'medium', 'Architecture and materials', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

