-- Forests of the Tide
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-mangrove', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Forests of the Tide', 'Why mangroves matter, and why replanting them often fails',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "Along many tropical and subtropical coastlines, where the land meets the sea, grow forests unlike any others. Mangroves are trees and shrubs adapted to life in the intertidal zone, the strip of shore that is flooded at high tide and exposed at low tide. Few plants can tolerate such conditions, which combine salt water, shifting mud and soil so waterlogged that it contains almost no oxygen. Mangroves cope in a variety of ways. Some species prevent most of the salt from entering at their roots, while others absorb it and then excrete it through special glands in their leaves, which may be seen glistening with salt crystals. Many species also produce aerial roots called pneumatophores, which grow upwards out of the mud like pencils and allow the root system to obtain oxygen from the air."}, {"label": "B", "text": "These unusual forests provide services of great value. Their dense tangle of roots slows incoming waves, traps sediment and reduces coastal erosion, and it can lessen the impact of storm surges on the communities behind them. The sheltered water among the roots serves as a nursery for young fish, crabs and shrimp, many of which later move offshore and support commercial fisheries. Mangroves also store remarkable amounts of carbon. Because their waterlogged soils decay very slowly, dead roots and leaves accumulate over centuries, and most of the carbon held by a mangrove forest is found in the soil rather than in the trees themselves. This ''blue carbon'', as carbon stored in coastal ecosystems is known, has attracted growing attention. For local people, mangroves also supply timber for building, firewood and food."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Despite these benefits, a substantial proportion of the world''s mangroves has been lost since the middle of the twentieth century. The single largest cause in many countries has been conversion to aquaculture, particularly ponds for farming shrimp, much of which is exported. Forests have also been cleared for rice fields and other agriculture, for ports and housing, and for coastal tourism. The consequences are often felt years later. Shrimp ponds typically become less productive after a few years, as disease and pollution build up, and many are then abandoned, leaving behind bare, enclosed areas of mud that no longer receive regular tidal flooding. Communities that have lost their mangroves may find themselves more exposed to storms, and fishers may find that their catches decline as nursery habitats disappear."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Governments and conservation organisations have responded with large replanting programmes, but many of these have failed. A common mistake has been to plant seedlings on open mudflats, which are attractive because they are not owned or used by anyone, but which lie lower on the shore than mangroves naturally grow. There, the seedlings are submerged for too long by each tide, and most of them die within a few years. Other projects have planted a single species over large areas, producing plantations that lack the variety of natural forests. Part of the problem lies in how success has been measured. When projects are judged by the number of seedlings planted, there is a strong incentive to plant as many as possible, wherever space is available, rather than to plant them where they are likely to survive."}, {"label": "E", "text": "A different approach, often described as ecological restoration, starts by asking why mangroves are absent from a site. In many cases the answer is that the natural flow of the tide has been blocked. By reopening channels blocked by the walls of abandoned ponds and restoring the tidal flow, restoration teams allow the seeds of nearby mangroves, known as propagules, to float in on the tide and establish themselves naturally. The trees that result are suited to the site, and the method is usually cheaper than growing and planting large numbers of seedlings. Experience also shows that the involvement of local communities is essential. Where people hold secure tenure rights over the land and share in the benefits of the forest, they have strong reasons to protect it; where they do not, restored areas are often cleared again."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Tree-planting targets are appealing to donors and politicians, because they produce impressive numbers that are easy to announce. In my view, they are also misleading. The number of seedlings planted tells us almost nothing about whether a forest has been restored; the measure that matters is how many trees are still alive and growing several years later. We should also be clear that protecting existing mangroves is far more cost-effective than attempting to restore them once they have been lost, because a mature forest provides its full range of benefits immediately. Carbon credit schemes, which pay for the carbon stored by protected or restored forests, could provide valuable funding, but only if a fair share of the money reaches the communities who live alongside the mangroves and bear the cost of leaving them intact."}]'::jsonb,
     802, 'medium', 'Ecology and conservation', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

