-- The Pen and the Keyboard
INSERT INTO reading_passages
    (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, passage_slot)
VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-handwriting', 'ielts-2026-01', 'The Pen and the Keyboard', 'Does the way we write affect the way we learn?',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "In lecture halls and classrooms around the world, the sound of pens on paper has increasingly been replaced by the tapping of keys. Many university students now take notes on laptops or tablets, and in some school systems the time devoted to teaching handwriting has been reduced, with joined-up or cursive writing dropped from the curriculum altogether. The reasons are easy to understand. Most adults do the majority of their writing on keyboards, typed notes are legible and easy to search, and digital files can be shared and stored without limit. Yet the change has prompted a lively debate among psychologists and educators. Does the way in which we write affect the way in which we learn? A growing body of research suggests that the answer may be yes, although it is rather more complicated than either side of the debate often admits."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Much of the recent discussion stems from a widely cited study published in 2014. Researchers asked students to watch recorded lectures and take notes either by hand or on a laptop, and then tested them on what they had learned. The laptop users took considerably more notes, but a large proportion of what they wrote was a word-for-word record of the lecture; they tended to transcribe rather than think. Students writing by hand could not keep up with the speaker, so they were forced to select the most important points, summarise them and paraphrase them in their own words. On factual questions, the two groups performed similarly. On conceptual questions, which required students to understand and apply ideas, those who had written by hand performed better. The researchers concluded that the extra mental processing required by handwriting benefited learning."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Later studies, however, have produced mixed results. Several attempts to repeat the experiment found smaller differences between the two groups, and some found no significant difference at all. One explanation is that the original study did not allow students to review their notes before the test. In real courses, students usually return to their notes when preparing for examinations, and in that situation a fuller record may be an advantage rather than a drawback: a student cannot revise material that was never written down. The benefit of handwriting may therefore depend on how notes are used afterwards, as well as on how they are produced. It is also possible that students who are taught to summarise on a laptop, rather than transcribe, gain the same advantages as those who write by hand."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The evidence regarding young children is more consistent. Learning to form letters by hand appears to help children recognise them. In several experiments, children who practised writing letters by hand were later better at identifying those letters than children who practised by typing or by tracing them. One plausible explanation is that producing a letter by hand requires a child to attend closely to its shape, and each attempt produces a slightly different version, which may help the child learn what all versions have in common. Brain imaging studies have added to this picture. After practising handwriting, children showed more activity in brain areas associated with reading when they looked at letters than children who had practised in other ways. For young children, at least, handwriting seems to be a foundation for reading rather than merely a way of recording words."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Practical considerations also affect the choice between pen and keyboard. A laptop connected to the internet offers constant distraction in the form of messages, social media and websites unrelated to the lesson, and studies have found that students who switch between tasks during lectures remember less. Nor is the effect confined to the user: students seated behind someone browsing on a laptop can also be distracted by the screen in front of them. On the other hand, for some students typing is not a convenience but a necessity. Conditions such as dyspraxia, which affects the coordination of movement, can make handwriting slow, tiring and difficult to read, and for these students a keyboard allows them to record information at a speed that would otherwise be impossible."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The debate has too often been presented as a straightforward choice between pen and keyboard, as though one tool were simply better than the other. In my view, this misses the point. What matters most is not the tool but the kind of thinking it encourages: a student who selects, summarises and questions will learn more than one who merely copies, whatever device is in their hands. For this reason, I would oppose the growing tendency of some lecturers to ban laptops from their classes. Such bans penalise the students who depend on keyboards, and they treat a problem of study habits as though it were a problem of technology. Young children are a different case. The evidence that handwriting supports early reading is strong enough that schools should continue to teach it, even as keyboards become ever more central to adult life."}]'::jsonb,
     824, 'medium', 'Education and psychology', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

