VALUES
    ('rp-ir67-bicycle', 'ielts-2026-01', 'Two Wheels Forward', 'How the bicycle reached its modern form',
     '[{"label": "A", "text": "The bicycle is so familiar that it is easy to forget how long it took to arrive at its modern form. Its story is usually traced to 1817, when the German inventor Karl Drais demonstrated a two-wheeled ''running machine'' in Mannheim. The machine had a wooden frame, a steerable front wheel and a padded seat, but no pedals: the rider sat astride it and pushed against the ground with both feet, gliding forward between steps. Some historians have linked the invention to the failed harvests of 1816, which caused a shortage of oats and led to the deaths of many horses, although the connection remains a matter of debate. Drais''s machine enjoyed a brief fashion among wealthy young men, but it was awkward on rough roads, and some cities banned it from pavements after collisions with pedestrians."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Half a century passed before the next major step. In the 1860s, French makers began attaching pedals and cranks directly to the front wheel, creating the velocipede. For the first time, a rider could propel the machine without touching the ground. The early velocipedes, however, were built with heavy iron frames and wooden wheels surrounded by iron tyres, and on the cobbled streets of the period they gave such a jarring ride that they became popularly known as ''boneshakers''. There was also a mechanical limitation. Because the pedals turned the front wheel directly, each turn of the pedals moved the machine forward by exactly the circumference of that wheel, which meant that the only way to go faster was to pedal faster or to make the wheel larger."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Designers chose the second option. During the 1870s, the front wheel grew steadily larger, until in some machines it was taller than a man, while the rear wheel shrank to a small support. This was the high-wheeler, later nicknamed the penny-farthing after two British coins of very different sizes. Lighter wire-spoked wheels and solid rubber tyres made it faster and more comfortable than the boneshaker, and it became popular for racing and long-distance touring. But it was also dangerous. The rider sat high above the front wheel, and a sudden stop, caused by a stone or a hole in the road, could throw him forward over the handlebars, an accident known as ''taking a header''. Riding a high-wheeler therefore remained largely a pastime for athletic young men who could afford both the machine and the risk."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The solution was the safety bicycle, which appeared in the 1880s. It had two wheels of similar size and a chain that transmitted power from the pedals to the rear wheel. Gearing between the chain wheel and the rear wheel allowed a small wheel to travel as far with each turn of the pedals as a large one, so there was no longer any need for a giant front wheel. The Rover, produced by the English manufacturer John Kemp Starley in 1885, is often regarded as the first commercially successful design of this type. Three years later, the Scottish inventor John Boyd Dunlop developed a practical pneumatic tyre, originally to make his son''s tricycle more comfortable. Air-filled tyres transformed the ride, and as mass production spread, the price of bicycles fell steadily."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The result was a cycling boom in the 1890s, and its social effects were considerable. For the first time, ordinary working people could travel several miles cheaply and independently. The bicycle had particular significance for women, who gained a new freedom of movement. Long skirts and tight corsets were unsuitable for cycling, and the movement for ''rational dress'' encouraged women to wear more practical clothing, including divided skirts and loose trousers known as bloomers. The American campaigner for women''s rights Susan B. Anthony famously claimed that cycling had done more than anything else to free women. Cyclists also became an influential political force. In both Britain and the United States, they campaigned for better roads, and many improvements that motorists later enjoyed were first demanded by cyclists, before cars were common."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The history of the bicycle is often told as a series of inventions by great individuals, but this is misleading. The machine evolved through decades of trial and error by many makers, most of whose names are forgotten, and the successful design emerged from the failures of its predecessors as much as from any single moment of genius. The history also carries a lesson for today. The current revival of cycling in many cities owes less to improvements in the bicycle itself than to the provision of safe, connected cycle lanes; in my view, infrastructure matters far more than technology. Electric bicycles are a welcome development, because they make cycling possible for older and less athletic people and for longer or hillier journeys. But cities should remember that cyclists were once among the loudest voices demanding good roads, and they should listen to them again."}]'::jsonb,
     809, 'medium', 'History and transport', ARRAY['IELTS Reading', 'Original practice'], 'custom')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

